# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Line endings — keep Linux scripts LF on EVERY platform, including Windows.
#
#  Why this file exists (do not remove):
#  These shell scripts run directly on Linux / WSL / SteamOS, and are often
#  shared as raw files (e.g. posted to Discord) and run straight from the
#  download. Git's core.autocrlf=true converts text to CRLF on a Windows
#  checkout, which turns the shebang into `#!/bin/bash<CR>`. Linux then fails
#  to run the script with:
#        cannot execute: required file not found
#  because it looks for an interpreter literally named "/bin/bash\r".
#
#  `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree regardless of OS or autocrlf, so
#  editing the source on Windows can never reintroduce CRLF into a script.
#  (Future extensionless scripts — e.g. a launcher with no .sh — must be
#  added here explicitly by path, since these globs match by extension.)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.zsh   text eol=lf
*.ksh   text eol=lf
*.fish  text eol=lf

# The committed byte snapshot of what `composegen.render()` writes (contract
# A16). `write_plan()` writes those files with `newline="\n"` on every OS, so
# the snapshot is only a BYTE snapshot while the checkout holds LF — a Windows
# checkout under core.autocrlf=true would otherwise grow CRLF and the phrase
# "byte for byte" in `test_composegen.py` would be resting on Python's
# universal-newline translation rather than on the bytes.
pylauncher/tests/data/wotlk-rendered/*.yml  text eol=lf

# The source patch `patch-sources` applies to a CMaNGOS checkout, and the
# pre-/post-image fixtures `test_patch.py` proves it against. The upstream
# files are LF, `git.py` clones with core.autocrlf=false so the checkout is LF
# on every platform, and the tests assert the applier's output equals the
# bytes `git apply` produced on Linux -- a CRLF checkout of either side would
# turn that byte equality into a line-ending coincidence, and a CRLF patch is
# not the file the upstream report attaches.
pylauncher/catalog/installers/shared/cmangos/patches/*.patch  text eol=lf
pylauncher/tests/fixtures/cmangos-vmap-8ec338a1/**            text eol=lf

# The upstream issue draft carries that same patch inline, and the fence is
# what a maintainer copies out and runs `git apply` on -- so the file is a
# patch carrier and not prose, and it is pinned for the reason the line above
# is. Measured on m910q 2026-09-05, the fence extracted from this file:
#   LF   -- `git apply --check` exits 0 on mangos-classic 8ec338a1 and
#           mangos-tbc f82e7d67 (the two revs the catalog pins) and on both
#           trees' current origin/master (9b682be6, 46d9a78d).
#   CRLF -- exit 1 on all four, `error: patch failed:
#           contrib/vmap_extractor/vmapextract/gameobject_extract.cpp:24`.
# The committed blob was always LF, so what GitHub serves has always applied;
# the CRLF copy is the one a Windows checkout puts in front of the owner, and
# he is the person who posts it.
pyplan/upstream-cmangos-doodad-issue.md                       text eol=lf
